## Editorial Execution (full ruleset — applies to any new copy: title, meta, new sections, rewritten/trimmed passages)

### Images — reuse only, never generate new
Every image referenced in an edit or rewrite must be an existing file already present in the source `.md`, same path, same filename. Never invent, generate, or propose a new image file. When trimming a section that had its own image, either:
- carry the image to a new section that legitimately covers similar ground, or
- state clearly that the image is now orphaned (has no home in the trimmed page) and name it explicitly, so it can be reused elsewhere or left alone — never drop it silently without saying so.
When restructuring into cards, link previews, or other visual containers, prefer real images already in the post over plain text links — but only using images that already exist in that post's own image set unless told otherwise.

### Voice
Senior beauty editor, Byrdie/Allure register. Knowledgeable, warm, authoritative, never breathless. Trust the reader. Curated point of view — what's included/cut/emphasized IS the voice.

### AEO/GEO — answer-first discipline
- Any new section addressing a real query (B-bucket) opens with a direct 2-3 sentence answer immediately after the heading — declarative, no hedging, extractable out of context.
- Named entities (techniques, products, finishes) get a clear declarative definition on first real mention in new/rewritten copy.
- No hook, story beat, or editorial flourish precedes the direct answer in AEO-relevant copy.

### American English
color, recognize, -ize not -ise, -or not -our. Non-negotiable.

### Banned — hard, zero exceptions
- **Long em dash (—)** anywhere. Replace with comma / new sentence / parentheses / "but" / restructure.
- Ellipsis for dramatic effect.
- Mid-sentence bolding for emphasis (bold is reserved for primary keyword mentions only, and only if the page already uses that convention — check the `.md` file).
- Constructions: "X is more than just Y," "Not only X but also Y," "The result? [word]," rhetorical-question transitions, "This means that...," "It is worth noting that...," "It is important to remember that..."
- Transitions: Furthermore, Moreover, Additionally, In conclusion, To summarise, In summary, Overall, Thus, Hence, Consequently, Nevertheless.
- Vocabulary: Elevate/elevates, Effortless(ly), Stunning, Game-changing, Transformative, Dive into/delve into/explore, Crucial/essential/vital (overused), Seamlessly, Perfect for/ideal for (as openers), Unlock, Leverage, Curated (generic), Holistic, Innovative, Cutting-edge, Empower(ing), Journey (metaphorical).
- Structural: every paragraph ending in a summary sentence, uniform section length, bullet lists where prose reads better, three consecutive sentences of identical length/rhythm.

### Sentence rhythm
Unpredictable by design. Short lands like fact, longer builds, short again. Never three short or three long in a row.

### Opening technique (for any new section/rewritten opening)
Pick from: The Claim, The Specific Detail, The Reframe, The Tension, The Observation, For the Reader, The Earned Fragment. Never restate the heading as a sentence. Never open with "When it comes to...," "One of the most...," "If you're looking for...," "Here's the thing," or similar banned openers.

### Human voice
Confident editorial judgment and house opinion, freely used ("this is the one shape that photographs better than it wears"). Never fabricate a specific first-person testing claim ("I tried this," "in my experience") unless the existing page content already establishes that voice — check before adding it.

### Trimming (C2 fix)
When re-centering a page: cut or substantially rework the passage causing the mismatch, don't just soften it. The goal is the page no longer plausibly ranks for the off-topic query. Keep the edit as small as it can be while still being effective — don't gut unrelated sections.

